---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import KeyTree from '../components/KeyTree.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="Security" description="How Aegis v2 protects your vault: Argon2id, XChaCha20-Poly1305, hybrid Ed25519 and ML-DSA-65 signatures, hybrid X25519 and ML-KEM-768 sharing, a published threat model, open source, and a responsible disclosure policy.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Security" title="Zero-knowledge, <em>documented.</em>" lede="This page is the same material a reviewer would read in the repository, in plain language. Every claim links to the code or document that backs it.">
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${site.docsUrl}/THREAT_MODEL.md`}>Threat model</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={`${site.docsUrl}/CRYPTO.md`}>Cryptography</a>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span>
        <h2 style="margin-top:.5rem">One passphrase, one key hierarchy</h2>
        <p>Your passphrase is stretched with Argon2id, at 64 MiB or more, into a wrapping key. That key unwraps a random 256-bit root secret that exists only in memory while the vault is open, and every other key is derived from it with HKDF-SHA256, salted with the vault's id.</p>
        <p>Changing the passphrase re-wraps the root secret only. Rotating your keys mints a new root secret, increments the vault's key epoch and replaces the sync and share identities, and it never happens silently.</p>
        <p>A Recovery Kit wraps the same root secret under an independent recovery secret that Aegis generates and never stores. A normal backup is different: it carries a data snapshot under its own key and restores as a new identity, so an old backup can never become your live vault.</p>
        <p>In the browser, all of this runs in WebAssembly compiled from the <code>common</code> Rust crate, behind a strict Content Security Policy with no remote scripts. Sealed blobs live in IndexedDB. No key material is written to disk unencrypted.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tree"><KeyTree /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead eyebrow="Cryptography">Suites, <em>version two.</em></SectionHead>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Suite</th><th>Used for</th><th>Algorithms</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono">0x0002 Core</td><td>Vault, audit log, backups, Recovery Kit</td><td>Argon2id, XChaCha20-Poly1305, HKDF-SHA256</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">0x0003 Sync</td><td>Multi-device revisions and identity transitions</td><td>Ed25519 <strong>and</strong> ML-DSA-65, both must verify</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">0x0004 Share</td><td>Sealing one entry to another user</td><td>X25519 <strong>and</strong> ML-KEM-768, combined, plus hybrid signatures</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">0x0001 Legacy</td><td>Version 0.1 vaults</td><td>Decode and migrate only. Never written by v2.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3 class="sub">Passphrase stretching</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Argon2id memory</td><td>64 MiB floor, 128 MiB preferred</td><td>Chosen per device; 256 MiB cap. Lower profiles cannot be generated.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Iterations, parallelism</td><td>3, 1</td><td>Caps of 8 and 4. Work factor is checked before allocation.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Passphrase floor</td><td>12 characters</td><td>A short denylist of obviously weak strings is rejected.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">Every external file starts with a magic string and version that are checked before any parsing: <code>AEGIS_VAULT_V2</code>, <code>AEGIS_BACKUP_V2</code>, <code>AEGIS_RECOVERY_V2</code>, <code>AEGIS_SYNC_V2</code>. A malformed v2 object is never retried as v1. Post-quantum primitives are pinned to FIPS 203 (ML-KEM-768) and FIPS 204 (ML-DSA-65) and checked against the official test vectors in CI.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SectionHead eyebrow="Threat model">What we protect, <em>and what we do not.</em></SectionHead>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table">
          <thead><tr><th>Adversary</th><th>Protected</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Network observer or malicious relay peer</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>Sees ciphertext and coarse metadata: size and timing. Forging a revision needs both Ed25519 and ML-DSA-65 broken.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Server or peer hosting sync state</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>Untrusted. It validates signatures and stores bytes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rinse Repeat Labs</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>We hold no key that opens a vault. A subpoena yields ciphertext.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stolen locked device</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>At-rest AEAD. Offline strength is passphrase entropy plus Argon2id at 64 MiB or more.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stolen backup file</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>An offline attack on that backup's passphrase only. The backup never contains the live root secret.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stolen Recovery Kit without its secret</td><td class="yes">Yes</td><td>The kit holds a wrapped root secret and no passwords. The recovery secret is never persisted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Other websites in the same browser</td><td class="part">Mostly</td><td>Origin isolation. A passphrase typed into a phishing copy of the UI is not protected.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Malicious update to the Aegis UI</td><td class="part">Partial</td><td>Strict CSP, self-hosted scripts and published hashes let you verify; a hostile UI can still read an unlocked vault.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Long-term harvest of ciphertext against a future crypto break</td><td class="part">Reduced</td><td>Sync and shares require breaking a classical and a post-quantum scheme together. Storage stays classical AEAD.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stolen unlocked device or memory dump</td><td class="no">No</td><td>The classic password-manager limit. Lock the vault when you step away.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Coercion or duress</td><td class="no">No</td><td>Out of scope.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">The complete model, including logging policy and sharing risks, is in <a href={`${site.docsUrl}/THREAT_MODEL.md`}>THREAT_MODEL.md</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="wrap three">
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Open source</h3>
        <p>Every component is public under MIT or Apache-2.0: the crypto core, the browser vault, the sync contract, the Freenet delegate and the documentation.</p>
        <p><a href={site.repoUrl}>github.com/imcmurray/Aegis</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Reproducible builds</h3>
        <p>Each release publishes blake3 hashes for the WASM modules. Build from the tagged commit and compare. Instructions are on the <a href="/download#verify">download page</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" id="disclosure">
        <h3>Responsible disclosure</h3>
        <p>Found a vulnerability? Email <a href={`mailto:${site.securityEmail}`}>{site.securityEmail}</a>. We acknowledge within two business days, keep you informed, and credit you in the release notes if you want. Please give us reasonable time to fix before publishing. Machine-readable details are in <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>What v2 was checked against</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Adversarial design review of the frozen v2 contract and every implementation phase</li>
            <li>42 of 42 browser scenarios in Chromium and Firefox: backup and restore, Recovery Kit, rotation, hybrid share, old-epoch rejection, local sync</li>
            <li>79.8 million fuzzer inputs across seven parser surfaces with no crashes, hangs or memory exhaustion</li>
            <li>FIPS 203 and FIPS 204 test vectors, frozen v1 fixtures, RustSec and cargo-deny in CI</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>What is not claimed</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Formal verification, or absolute security</li>
            <li>An independent professional audit. One is planned before Teams leaves early access, and the report will be linked here when it exists.</li>
            <li>Protection of an unlocked vault against a memory dump</li>
            <li>Clickjacking protection on GitHub Pages, where CSP is a meta tag and <code>frame-ancestors</code> is not enforced</li>
            <li>Detecting a stale Recovery Kit on an empty install, or a coherent older sync snapshot when no newer counter was kept</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta title="Read the code, then <em>trust the vault.</em>" primary={{ label: 'View on GitHub', href: site.repoUrl }} secondary={{ label: 'Open the free vault', href: site.appUrl }} />
</Base>
<style>
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.1fr; gap: 4rem; align-items: center; }
  .tree { background: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line-dark); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1.75rem; }
  .three { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 2.5rem; }
  .grid-2 { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; }
  .grid-2 h3 { margin-top: 0; }
  .sub { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 2rem 0 .75rem; }
  .fine { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: .88rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .fine code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .85em; }
  .fine a { color: var(--accent); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .two, .three, .grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
